    // Create a queue to store the nodes to be visited
    Queue<int[]> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();

    // Add the starting node to the queue
    queue.add(start);

    // Loop until the queue is empty
    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
        // Get the next node from the queue
        int[] node = queue.remove();

        // Check if the node is already visited
        if (visitedNodes.contains(node[0] + "," + node[1])) {
            continue;
        }

        // Mark the node as visited
        visitedNodes.add(node[0] + "," + node[1]);

        // Check if the current node is the end node
        if (node[0] == end[0] && node[1] == end[1]) {
            return "can be travelled";
        }

        // Get the paths from the current node
        int[][] pathsFromCurrentNode = paths[node[0] - 1][node[1] - 1];

        // Loop through the paths
        for (int i = 0; i < pathsFromCurrentNode.length; i++) {
            // Check if the path is valid
            if (pathsFromCurrentNode[i][0] != 0 && pathsFromCurrentNode[i][1] != 0) {
                // Add the path to the queue
                queue.add(new int[] { pathsFromCurrentNode[i][0], pathsFromCurrentNode[i][1] });
            }
        }
    }

    // If the queue is empty and the end node has not been visited, return "cannot be travelled"
    return "cannot be travelled";
}
```

Note: In this solution, we use a breadth-first search (BFS) algorithm to traverse the n-teresting space. We create a set to store the visited nodes, a queue to store the nodes to be visited, and a flag to indicate if the end node has been visited. In the main loop, we pop the next node from the queue, check if it is already visited, and if not, mark it as visited and check if it is the end node. If it is, return "can be travelled". Then, we get the paths from the current node, loop through the paths, and check if the path is valid. If it is, we add the path to the queue. If the queue is empty and the end node has not been visited, return "cannot be travelled".

