public int maxUniqueSplit(String s) {
    int[] counts = new int[26];
    for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
        counts[s.charAt(i) - 'a']++;
    }
    int numUnique = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
        if (counts[i] != 0) {
            numUnique++;
        }
    }
    int maxUnique = Math.min(numUnique, s.length());
    for (int i = 1; i < s.length() - 1; i++) {
        if (counts[s.charAt(i) - 'a'] == 1) {
            int maxUniqueLeft = maxUniqueSplit(s.substring(0, i)) + 1;
            int maxUniqueRight = maxUniqueSplit(s.substring(i + 1));
            maxUnique = Math.max(maxUnique, maxUniqueLeft + maxUniqueRight);
        }
    }
    return maxUnique;
}
```
This method uses a trick to make use of the coupled recurrence relationship between the maximum numbers of unique substrings in two non-overlapping parts of a string and the maximum number of unique substrings in the whole string. At each position in the string, we try to partition the string at that position and calculate the maximum number of unique substrings in both parts. Then we combine the maximum numbers of unique substrings in both parts with the number of unique substrings in the whole string to get the maximum number of unique substrings in the whole string. We return the maximum number of unique substrings in the whole string as the result.

This method has a time complexity of O(n^2) where n is the length of the input string, and a space complexity of O(n) since we need to store the counts of character frequencies in the array `counts`.

