1. The area of the bar itself, i.e., the product of its height and width.
2. The area of a rectangle formed by one of the bars and a shorter bar to its left, i.e., the product of the two bars' heights and the shorter bar's width.
3. The area of a rectangle formed by one of the bars and a shorter bar to its right, i.e., the product of the two bars' heights and the shorter bar's width.

Therefore, we can solve this problem by computing the maximum of the above expressions for each bar.
```java
public static int largestRectangleArea(int[] heights) {
    int n = heights.length;
    int[] leftMax = new int[n];
    int[] rightMax = new int[n];
    int maxArea = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        int height = heights[i];
        if (i == 0) {
            leftMax[i] = height;
        } else {
            leftMax[i] = Math.max(height, leftMax[i - 1]);
        }
        if (i == n - 1) {
            rightMax[i] = height;
        } else {
            rightMax[i] = Math.max(height, rightMax[i + 1]);
        }
        int width = Math.min(leftMax[i], rightMax[i]);
        maxArea = Math.max(maxArea, height * width);
    }
    return maxArea;
}
```
This solution has a time complexity of O(n) and a space complexity of O(n) for the arrays `leftMax` and `rightMax`.

